\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
A physical model for reed design
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Self-sustained conditions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Stored energy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Dissipation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
A flow-driven formulation of the reed--chamber coupling
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The bench drives the reed with a turbine feeding a chamber, and the
 accordion itself is driven by the bellows imposing a volume velocity.
 Neither imposes a pressure: the pressure is what results once the flow
 meets the impedance of the open reeds.
 The everyday evidence is familiar to any player -- at a constant bellows
 speed, opening a chord makes the pressure drop, whereas a pressure source
 would hold the pressure and simply double the flow.
 The model is therefore written with the flow as the input variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Four choices of formulation turn out to be decisive, and each of them was
 reached only after the corresponding mistake had been made and measured.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The state variable of the chamber is the pressure, not the volume.
 A rigid chamber has a fixed volume; what varies is the mass of air it
 holds.
 Using the volume as a state together with an adiabatic law made the
 pressure fall when air was injected, so that the chamber went into
 depression, the tongue was sucked against the plate and stayed closed
 99.2 per cent of the time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The opening is asymmetric.
 A free reed feeds in one air direction only, the other half-swing being
 closed by the leather valve -- which is precisely why blowing and drawing
 use two distinct reeds.
 A symmetric opening law modulates the slot twice per period and produces
 a note one octave too high, measured here as 470 Hz for a 220 Hz tongue.
 The asymmetry is not a refinement: it sets the pitch.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The open area is bounded on both sides: a clean closure, with the residual
 leak that every real plate has, and a saturation once the tongue has
 cleared the slot.
 A numerical floor of the kind used to keep a square root finite hides the
 closure instead of modelling it, and lets the tongue pass through its own
 plate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The modal damping matrix must be symmetric.
 The projection basis (uniform cantilever modes) is not the eigenbasis of
 a multi-section tongue, so the mass matrix carries about 43 per cent of
 off-diagonal coupling; the natural-looking product of the mass matrix by a
 diagonal of modal damping is then not symmetric, and such a matrix injects
 energy depending on the direction of motion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The chamber equation finally reads
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\[\frac{dp}{dt}=\frac{\gamma\,(P_{atm}+p)}{V_{0}}\left(q_{in}-q_{out}(p,y)-q_{reed}\right)\]
\end_layout

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where the last term is the volume swept by the tongue itself.
 That term deserves attention: for a tongue vibrating at 100 Hz with an
 amplitude of one millimetre it reaches about 1.2e-4 cubic metres per
 second, roughly twelve times the supply flow.
 The stability of the whole model is governed by it, not by the supply.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Predicting the threshold instead of searching for it
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Simulating for half a second and watching whether the amplitude decays is a
 slow and treacherous way to decide whether a reed starts.
 Linearising around the static equilibrium and reading the eigenvalues of
 the Jacobian answers the same question in about a millisecond, and answers
 it exactly: the sign of the largest real part decides stability, and its
 imaginary part gives the starting frequency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Carrying the linearisation through by hand on a single mode gives the
 condition for self-oscillation:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\[\frac{\partial q_{out}}{\partial y}\;>\;\gamma_{m}\,\frac{\gamma\,P_{atm}}{V_{0}}\,\frac{\partial q_{out}}{\partial p}\]
\end_layout

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the left, what sustains: the modulation of the flow by the motion of the
 tongue.
 On the right, what dissipates: the reaction of the air spring to the volume
 swept by the tongue.
 Written in terms of the chamber volume this becomes a lower bound on the
 effective acoustic volume, which is an order of magnitude rather than an
 equality -- the scalar estimate calls for 78 cubic centimetres where the
 full model already starts at 40.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The saturation trap
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This criterion carries a consequence that no amount of simulation had made
 visible.
 If the opening saturates -- the tongue blown clear of the slot -- then the
 derivative of the opening with respect to the tongue position vanishes,
 the left-hand side of the criterion goes to zero, and the sustaining term
 disappears whatever the pressure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That was exactly the situation: the tip settled at 0.649 mm against a
 saturation set at 0.4 mm.
 The tongue sat outside its own working range and no longer modulated
 anything.
 The same mechanism bounds the oscillation from above and produces, without
 being asked for it, the choking that every player knows when pushing too
 hard on the bellows.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Limit cycle and the impedance of the source
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An ideal flow source imposes its flow even when the tongue closes the slot.
 The pressure then spikes without bound -- 8550 Pa were measured -- and that
 spike drives the tongue harder still, so the amplitude grows indefinitely.
 A real turbine, like a real bellows, delivers less as the pressure rises,
 following a droop law q = q0 - p/R.
 It is this droop that bounds the amplitude.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With a finite source impedance the limit cycle converges: the peak-to-peak
 excursion of the tip settles at 1.1364 mm and stays there, an amplitude
 typical of a real accordion reed.
 The convergence takes about 1.5 s of simulated signal, which is worth
 stating: shorter runs wrongly suggest an unbounded amplitude.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The resistance R is not a quantity to be guessed.
 It is the slope of the pressure-flow characteristic of the source, and the
 existing sweep of the Section factor already produces the data needed to
 measure it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
A subcritical bifurcation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Starting from an established limit cycle and lowering the command, the
 oscillation persists below the starting threshold.
 The bifurcation is therefore subcritical, with a starting threshold of
 25.8 Pa, an extinction threshold of 19.4 Pa and a hysteresis ratio of about
 1.33.
 This is the behaviour of real reeds, and the two thresholds are exactly
 what an ascending then descending pressure ramp measures on the bench.
 Model and measurement now speak of the same quantity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Reaching that conclusion required care, and two wrong answers were obtained
 first.
 Restarting from an established cycle demands the exact state:
 reconstructing the modal state from the tip displacement alone is
 underdetermined and produced a systematic extinction, hence a spurious
 supercritical verdict.
 And the amplitude law in the square root of the distance to threshold,
 which was correctly measured, is the expected signature of a supercritical
 bifurcation but is necessary rather than sufficient: a weakly subcritical
 one shows the same law above threshold.
 Only the descent decides -- which is precisely why the bench ramp is run in
 both directions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
What the model now predicts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the weighted bass tongue of the reference geometry, whose first mode
 falls at 102.1 Hz:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
starting threshold 25.8 Pa, at 119 Hz;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
choking threshold 379.8 Pa;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
extinction threshold 19.4 Pa, hysteresis ratio 1.33;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
limit-cycle excursion of 0.96 mm at 1.5 times the threshold, growing as the
 square root of the distance to threshold;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
playing frequency constant at 118 Hz across the band, about 16 per cent
 above the first mode of the tongue -- the stiffening due to the air spring.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That last figure is directly useful: the ratio between the playing frequency
 and the free tongue frequency depends on the effective acoustic volume, so
 measuring the shift measures the volume.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
A note on method
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Three mistakes made during this work are worth recording, because each was
 caught by a measurement rather than by reasoning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
A spectrum judged poor -- two harmonics -- was in fact rich, with six to
 twelve.
 Two errors compounded: the chamber pressure was examined where a free reed
 radiates through the modulated flow (and, in the far field, through its
 derivative), and the fundamental was detected by taking the strongest
 partial, which differentiation had moved to the fourth harmonic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Slot inertance, the most natural candidate for the missing phase lag, was
 eliminated by measurement: it changed the growth rate from -46.8 to -47.5
 per second, that is, not at all.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
A parameter sweep appeared to show self-oscillation until it was noticed
 that reversing the sign of the reed coupling changed the result by less
 than one part in a thousand -- proof that the coupling was not driving
 anything, and that what was being measured was a decaying transient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The common thread is that a model which merely looks plausible is worth
 nothing, and that the cheapest instrument against self-deception is a test
 whose outcome would be different if the hypothesis were false.
\end_layout

