\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
The hole.
\series default
 A tongue needs three to five times its effective section to breathe, 60 to 100 mm². The rest of the hole serves the acoustics of the chamber and the way out of the sound. A hole of 12 by 12 mm halves the pressure force; with 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset
 = 1 and a peak of 1.5 kPa it gives 72 g at mid-stroke and 80 g at the lift-off.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
The real peak pressure.
\series default
 Between 1.5 and 3 kPa the preload goes from 0.84 to 1.38 N. A jazz player who plays softly may never pass 1.5 kPa; a U-tube manometer says so in an afternoon.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
r at most 1.
\series default
 Travel 5 mm, lift 5 to 5.5 mm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
A long, soft spring
\series default
, rising by 15 to 25 % over the lift, not 100 %.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
The efficiency
\series default
: turning, not sliding (below).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
The seat
\series default
: a flat seat and a supple leather seal with 0.3 N; a warped seat asks for 0.6 N, which the finger pays.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Four ways to cheat the wind, and why three of them fail
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Can the pressure force be compensated, the way a sash window is balanced by its counterweight? Four ideas come naturally. Figure 
